This is a rely from Janet Walz to a question I asked that may be 
of general interest to all hackers:

> >A quick question.  We have several architectures here (Masscomp, Sun3,
> >SPARC, HP, ...) and would like to have a shared playground.  It
> >appears though that not all files can be shared and that save files
> >and bones files in particular are machine-dependant.  Is this correct?
> 
> Yes, save and bones files are machine dependent (they're smaller and
> faster that way).  The compiled special levels and the long at the
> beginning of rumors may also be problems, but they're more likely to be
> compatible.
> 
> >Any tips on this subject would be gratefully received,
> 
> Usually people seem to create separate directories for each architecture
> and then link the compatible files (like the scoreboard and most of the
> help files) into the various directories.

What I was angling at was a method by which the same save file could
be used on any machine, so in a multi-architecture network you could
log in anywhere and continue with the same game that you had before,
whichever machine it was on.  And maybe to have the same special
levels and endgame on each machine too.  

Just how much would the overhead be to store saved games/bones/etc
in plain ASCII or whatever to give this compatability ??

